{"record": "turn", "turn": 2, "seconds": 3.106, "response_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "stop_reason": "tool_use", "stop_details": null, "blocks": ["tool_use"], "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 65721, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 2, "output_tokens": 317, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 0}, "service_tier": "standard", "speed": "standard"}, "tools": [{"id": "toolu_013UVH3LHiXVEG4cLCHJqQGw", "name": "submit_answer", "arguments": {"answer": "In the SFDM benchmark (m_ψ = 47.75 GeV, m_h2 = 14.325 GeV, r = 0.3), the paper gives these h2 branching fractions: Br(h2→b b̄) = 0.7910, Br(h2→c c̄) = 0.1281 and Br(h2→τ⁺τ⁻) = 0.0797. The 4b final state accounts for about 62.6% of h2h2 decays. This matches 0.791² ≈ 0.626. The remaining combinations, such as 2b2c and 2b2τ, make up the rest.", "found": true, "evidence": [{"arxiv_id": "2610.26648v1", "section_id": "7.2"}]}, "status": "answer", "error_kind": null, "seconds": 0.0}]}
